---
title: "Presence Is Different"
description: "Clicks get botted and reach gets bought. Showing up is the one signal a budget cannot buy, and until now nobody could count it."
date: 2026-10-03
draft: true
---

For fifteen years I watched the same thing from two sides. In clubs and on tour, the room was full of people who had travelled, queued and paid to be there. In boardrooms, brands spent fortunes trying to reach people like them, and could rarely name one.

Both sides were counting the wrong thing. Marketing runs on signals that are cheap to produce: clicks, follows, impressions. Presence, actually turning up, is the one that matters most, and the one nobody recorded. Here is why I think it is different.

## It is expensive

Turning up costs time, travel and money. That is exactly why it means something. At Kasina × Crocs in Seoul, the invited crowd stayed with the game for eight minutes.

## It cannot be faked

Clicks get botted. Followers get bought. Impressions get distributed. You cannot buy ten thousand attendances. On the Navinder Nangla drop at END., every entrant came through the tag in a tee they owned, and the duplicate and fraud rate was 0%.

## Spend cannot replace it

You can buy reach. You cannot buy attendance. Half of Mulaa Joans' Portal visits came from a physical tap, with no paid media behind them. It is the one audience a media budget cannot grow, which is why a sponsor should pay differently for it.

## It is scarce

Five hundred thousand followers, four hundred attendees. The four hundred are worth more per head, and until now nobody could isolate them. With H.LLS, the fans who showed up most were almost all people who had tapped an object. Fewer people, better people.

## What changes when you can count it

None of this is news to anyone who has run a night. What was missing was a record: something that kept the arrival, the consent and what happened next, and belonged to the brand rather than to a platform.

That is what we build at DEPARTMNT. A tap on a wristband, a garment or a wall opens a page in the browser. No app, ever. The person chooses what to share, and the brand finally knows who was in the room.

The room was always the asset. Now it can be counted.
